SQL  Injection: Querying the Database Type and Version (MySQL & Microsoft SQL Server)
Overview

In this PortSwigger Web Security Academy lab, I explored how an attacker can use SQL injection to identify the type and version of the underlying database management system (DBMS).

Rather than immediately extracting sensitive data, the objective of this lab was to fingerprint the target database by retrieving its version information.

Understanding the database platform is an important step during a security assessment because different database systems support different SQL syntax, functions, and exploitation techniques.

bjective

Retrieve the database type and version information from a vulnerable application using SQL injection.  

Skills Practiced
- SQL Injection
- Database fingerprinting
- Database enumeration
-Crafting SQL injection payloads
-Identifying MySQL and Microsoft SQL Server version information

Why This Matters
One of the first objectives after identifying a SQL injection vulnerability is determining the type of database running behind the application.
Knowing the database version enables an attacker to:

- Identify known vulnerabilities affecting that DBMS version.
- Craft database-specific SQL injection payloads.
-Select appropriate exploitation techniques.
-Better understand the target environment.
Database fingerprinting provides valuable information that can increase the effectiveness of subsequent attacks if the vulnerability is left unpatched.

Key Takeaways
- Different database management systems use different syntax for retrieving version information.
- Successful SQL injection is not limited to extracting application data; it can also reveal information about the underlying infrastructure.
-Identifying the database platform is often an early step in the exploitation process.

Security Recommendation To reduce the risk of SQL injection attacks:
 Use parameterized queries (prepared statements) instead of dynamically building SQL queries.
 Apply the principle of least privilege to database accounts.
- Avoid exposing database error messages or version information to end users.
- Validate and sanitize user input.
- Conduct regular security testing to identify injection vulnerabilities before attackers do.

